T1  - How are physical activity, sedentary behavior, and sleep related to mental health in adults with obesity
T3  - 32nd European Congress on Obesity, ECO 2025. Malaga Spain.
A1  - Aime A.
A1  - Petrin R.
A1  - Romain A.J.
A1  - Gagnon C.
A1  - Poirier R.
A1  - Ramos Caballero D.M.
A1  - Tordecilla Sanders A.
A1  - Trompetero Gonzalez A.C.
A1  - Garcia Laguna G.
A1  - Forest G.
A1  - Fermont L.
A1  - Baillot A.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: Physical activity is associated with better mental health in adults from the general population. However, the findings observed in the general population do not necessarily apply to adults with obesity, and the relationships between sedentary behavior, sleep, and mental health in this population remain to be elucidated. This systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to synthetize the literature that focuses on the relationship physical activity, sedentary behavior, and sleep have with mental health in adults with obesity. Method(s): An electronic search of five different databases was conducted until April 2024 to identify relevant studies. To be considered, experimental and observational studies had to include adults aged over 18 years with a body mass index >=30 kg/m2 in their sample. All selected studies focused at least on one of the components of the 24-hour movement continuum, i.e., physical activity, sedentary behaviors, or sleep. Mental health outcomes included depressive symptoms, anxiety, quality of life, eating disorders, disordered eating, body image, self-esteem, and social physical anxiety. Titles, abstracts and full texts were screened by independent reviewers, and conflicts were resolved by discussion. For experimental studies, meta-analyses were performed using continuous random effects and maximum likelihood estimation methods. Result(s): In total 2256 studies were found, and this number was reduced to 1548 after removing the duplicates. After screening, 66 were retained, out of which 60 focused exclusively on the association between physical activity and mental health outcomes. Sedentary behavior and sleep were very rarely considered, with only two and five observational studies found respectively. Quality of life was assessed in 70% of the studies, followed by depressive symptoms (26%), and anxiety (12%). The vast majority of both experimental and observational studies showed a positive association between physical activity and quality of life, but this association was not confirmed by the results of the meta-analysis. A positive association, partially supported by the meta-analysis results (k=4; Hedge's g=-0.62, 95%CI [-1.28; 0.04], p=0.06), was found between physical activity and depressive symptoms. Results were sparse and inconsistent for the other mental health outcomes. Conclusion(s): Although sedentary behavior and sleep are important complements to physical activity in lifestyle interventions, they are often overlooked in relation to mental health in adults with obesity. In addition, this study suggests that physical activity could be used to reduce depressive symptoms in adults with obesity, an association that has long been suggested but rarely empirically supported in this population.
